   \"status\": \"candidate\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"c3\",\n        \"tuples\": [[...]],\n        \"variables\": [...]\n      }\n    },\n    \"target\": {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:c3-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-6432\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-6432\", \"revision\": 1, \"state_revision\": 1},\n    {\"resource_id\": \"claim:c3-tuples\", \"revision\": 1, \"state_revision\": 1}\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\"shared-integrator\"],\n  \"reply_to\": \"csp-6432-c3-proposer-1\",\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"c2-reviewer\"\n}\n\nWe need to fill basis_refs with appropriate constraint IDs that support the claim. The claim is about c3 component. The constraints that directly involve i,j,k,l are c9, c10, c11, c12. Also the bridge constraint c18 involves f,i, which we need to check. The claim's tuple i=0 must satisfy c18 with some f. That forces f=3. So we need to ensure that f=3 is consistent with other constraints. That includes c5 (e,f), c6 (f,g), c17 (b,e). So we need to check those constraints as well. But the basis_refs likely include c9, c10, c11, c12, c18, maybe c5, c6, c17? The template example includes basis_refs: [\"c9\",\"c10\",\"c11\",\"c12\"] (as shown in the envelope template). That suggests that we can use those as basis references. The envelope template includes basis_refs exactly those four. So we can follow that. The envelope template includes basis_refs: [\"c9\", \"c10\", \"c11\", \"c12\"]. So we can keep that.\n\nThus the envelope we produce should match the template exactly, except we need to fill corrected_claim with the claim (maybe same as original). The envelope template includes corrected_claim with a different tuple [0,0,0,0] as a candidate. But we can choose to include the original claim as corrected_claim (maybe we can keep the original claim as candidate). However the template includes corrected_claim with a